1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,918
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,918 --> 00:00:08,365
This is chapter twenty-three: equals, hashCode and comparators.

3
00:00:08,815 --> 00:00:17,263
Every hash-based and sorted collection is built on four methods you write yourself: equals, hashCode, compareTo and compare.

4
00:00:17,263 --> 00:00:19,851
The collections trust them completely.

5
00:00:19,851 --> 00:00:23,121
When they break their contracts, nothing throws.

6
00:00:23,121 --> 00:00:29,253
Entries are silently lost, duplicated or dropped, and the bug shows up far from its cause.

7
00:00:30,153 --> 00:00:34,643
Four failures, all from collections working exactly as designed.

8
00:00:34,643 --> 00:00:39,766
Override equals but not hashCode, and a HashSet keeps two identical SKUs.

9
00:00:39,766 --> 00:00:46,011
Put the same two BigDecimals in a HashSet and a TreeSet, and you get two different sizes.

10
00:00:46,011 --> 00:00:50,501
A comparator written with subtraction overflows on large values.

11
00:00:50,501 --> 00:00:53,308
And a mutated map key strands its entry.

12
00:00:53,308 --> 00:00:54,782
None of these throws.

13
00:00:54,782 --> 00:00:56,606
Each has a one-line cause.

14
00:00:57,506 --> 00:01:01,098
Here's the fact that explains the BigDecimal result.

15
00:01:01,098 --> 00:01:07,661
Hash collections use two methods: hashCode to find the bucket, then equals to find the element.

16
00:01:08,111 --> 00:01:12,562
Sorted collections use only one: compareTo, or your comparator.

17
00:01:12,562 --> 00:01:14,681
They never call equals at all.

18
00:01:15,131 --> 00:01:22,068
And BigDecimal one point zero equals one point zero zero is false, because their scale differs.

19
00:01:22,068 --> 00:01:24,477
But compareTo says they're equal.

20
00:01:24,477 --> 00:01:28,785
So the HashSet sees two elements, and the TreeSet sees one.

21
00:01:29,685 --> 00:01:34,854
The one rule that breaks things: equal objects must have equal hash codes.

22
00:01:34,854 --> 00:01:36,042
That's mandatory.

23
00:01:36,492 --> 00:01:39,849
compareTo returning zero should agree with equals.

24
00:01:39,849 --> 00:01:41,730
That's strongly recommended.

25
00:01:41,730 --> 00:01:46,497
When it doesn't, hash and sorted collections disagree, like BigDecimal.

26
00:01:46,947 --> 00:01:49,136
But the converse isn't required.

27
00:01:49,136 --> 00:01:51,735
Unequal objects can share a hash code.

28
00:01:51,735 --> 00:01:55,566
That's just a collision, which every hash table handles.

29
00:01:56,016 --> 00:02:02,776
equals must be reflexive, symmetric, transitive and consistent, and equals of null is false.

30
00:02:02,776 --> 00:02:06,524
Use exactly the same fields in equals and hashCode.

31
00:02:06,524 --> 00:02:10,565
And for compareTo, only the sign of the result matters.

32
00:02:11,465 --> 00:02:15,074
If you hand-write equals, use the instanceof pattern.

33
00:02:15,074 --> 00:02:20,726
Null never matches a type pattern, so you get the null check and the cast for free.

34
00:02:20,726 --> 00:02:23,177
And use the same fields in hashCode.

35
00:02:23,627 --> 00:02:26,976
But for value types, the better answer is a record.

36
00:02:26,976 --> 00:02:34,068
It generates both methods from the same components, so they can never drift apart when someone adds a field.

37
00:02:34,068 --> 00:02:37,942
Hand-written equals should be the exception, with a reason.

38
00:02:38,842 --> 00:02:40,799
Here's the inheritance trap.

39
00:02:40,799 --> 00:02:42,407
Money compares amounts.

40
00:02:42,407 --> 00:02:45,972
TaxedMoney extends it and also compares a tax code.

41
00:02:45,972 --> 00:02:50,445
Now plain equals taxed is true, but taxed equals plain is false.

42
00:02:50,445 --> 00:02:57,295
Symmetry is broken, and whether a collection finds the object depends on which equals gets called.

43
00:02:57,745 --> 00:03:04,111
There's no way to extend an instantiable class, add a value component, and keep the contract.

44
00:03:04,111 --> 00:03:05,754
Use composition instead.

45
00:03:05,754 --> 00:03:09,793
A getClass check is symmetric, but breaks substitutability.

46
00:03:09,793 --> 00:03:13,489
Or make the class final, which records are by default.

47
00:03:14,389 --> 00:03:17,492
Never compute a comparison by subtraction.

48
00:03:17,492 --> 00:03:23,107
Subtract a negative from three billion paise, cast to int, and it overflows.

49
00:03:23,107 --> 00:03:26,801
The sign flips, and the biggest order sorts first.

50
00:03:26,801 --> 00:03:31,455
It passes every test with small numbers and fails on real data.

51
00:03:31,455 --> 00:03:34,336
Use comparingLong, or Long dot compare.

52
00:03:34,786 --> 00:03:37,287
And fix BigDecimal at the boundary.

53
00:03:37,287 --> 00:03:50,935
Normalise the scale in the record's compact constructor, so equals and compareTo always agree, and a map keyed by price doesn't treat ten point zero and ten point zero zero as different keys.

54
00:03:51,835 --> 00:03:55,488
Compose comparators with comparing and thenComparing.

55
00:03:55,488 --> 00:03:57,004
Careful with reversed.

56
00:03:57,004 --> 00:04:01,070
It reverses everything built so far, not just the last key.

57
00:04:01,070 --> 00:04:05,274
Put it at the end of the chain and you've reversed every key.

58
00:04:05,274 --> 00:04:09,478
To reverse just one, pass reverseOrder to that thenComparing.

59
00:04:09,928 --> 00:04:15,930
And always end with a unique tie-breaker, like the order ID, so the order is total.

60
00:04:15,930 --> 00:04:16,726
Here's why.

61
00:04:16,726 --> 00:04:21,065
A TreeSet uses the comparator as its definition of equality.

62
00:04:21,065 --> 00:04:28,296
Two orders with the same priority are the same element to it, so the second add is silently dropped.

63
00:04:29,196 --> 00:04:30,311
Three internals.

64
00:04:30,311 --> 00:04:35,885
HashMap compares the cached hashes first, and only calls equals on a hash match.

65
00:04:35,885 --> 00:04:45,431
So without a hashCode override, two equal keys get different identity hashes, land in different buckets, and equals is never even called.

66
00:04:45,881 --> 00:04:54,207
Objects dot hash is convenient but takes varargs, so it allocates an array and boxes primitives on every call.

67
00:04:54,207 --> 00:05:00,793
For hot keys, hand-roll it, or use a record, whose generated hashCode avoids the array.

68
00:05:01,243 --> 00:05:04,119
And keep doubles and arrays out of keys.

69
00:05:04,119 --> 00:05:13,540
Double's equals treats NaN as equal to itself and zero as different from negative zero, the opposite of the double-equals operator.

70
00:05:13,540 --> 00:05:19,653
Arrays compare by identity, which also silently breaks records with array components.

71
00:05:20,553 --> 00:05:21,772
Production rules.

72
00:05:21,772 --> 00:05:23,780
Use records for value types.

73
00:05:23,780 --> 00:05:30,736
If you hand-write equals, write hashCode too, with the same fields, using the instanceof pattern.

74
00:05:30,736 --> 00:05:33,963
Prefer composition to inheritance for values.

75
00:05:33,963 --> 00:05:35,899
Normalise BigDecimal scale.

76
00:05:35,899 --> 00:05:40,991
And build comparators with comparing, ending with a unique tie-breaker.

77
00:05:41,441 --> 00:05:44,338
Never override equals without hashCode.

78
00:05:44,338 --> 00:05:46,492
Never compare by subtraction.

79
00:05:46,492 --> 00:05:49,834
Never extend a value class and add to equals.

80
00:05:49,834 --> 00:05:52,286
Never use or mutate mutable keys.

81
00:05:52,286 --> 00:05:54,960
Keep doubles and arrays out of keys.

82
00:05:54,960 --> 00:06:01,942
And never assume a TreeSet and a HashSet agree, unless the ordering is consistent with equals.

83
00:06:02,842 --> 00:06:03,483
To recap.

84
00:06:03,483 --> 00:06:09,465
Hash collections use hashCode and equals, while sorted collections use only compare.

85
00:06:09,465 --> 00:06:12,385
Equal objects must have equal hash codes.

86
00:06:12,385 --> 00:06:16,018
Records generate both methods, so they can't drift.

87
00:06:16,018 --> 00:06:21,573
Use Long dot compare, never subtraction, and make orderings total for TreeSet.

88
00:06:22,023 --> 00:06:25,423
That completes the Collections section of the handbook.

89
00:06:25,423 --> 00:06:26,845
Next comes concurrency.

90
00:06:26,845 --> 00:06:28,081
Thanks for watching.
